   rollout   advantage    sum_t log p    A * sum_t log p
         0      +0.625       -282.547           -176.592
         1      -0.375       -253.557            +95.084
         2      +0.625       -119.706            -74.816
         3      -0.375       -147.554            +55.333
         4      -0.375       -198.650            +74.494
         5      +0.625       -288.984           -180.615
         6      -0.375       -258.508            +96.940
         7      -0.375       -142.490            +53.434

  num_valid (all rollouts) = 982
  pg_obj from chat_rl.py code path : -0.057778802
  pg_obj from  sum_i A_i*sum_t logp / num_valid : -0.057778794
  |difference| = 7.45e-09
  loss = -pg_obj = +0.057778802

  DERIVATION of -A * sum_t log p:
    maximise J = E_y~pi [ A(y) ].  REINFORCE: grad J = E[ A(y) * grad log pi(y|x) ]
    and log pi(y|x) = sum_t log p(y_t | y_<t, x)  (chain rule).
    So the surrogate whose gradient equals grad J is   sum_i A_i * sum_t log p_it,
    and since optimizers MINIMISE, the loss is its negative:  -A * sum_t log p.
    logp is obtained as -model(..., loss_reduction='none') because cross_entropy returns
    NLL; ignore_index=-1 already zeroes the masked positions, so no extra masking is needed.

==================================================================================================
4. NORMALIZATION: token-level (DAPO style), not sequence-level
==================================================================================================
  nanochat divides ONE total by num_valid summed over the whole group:
     pg = sum_i A_i * sum_t logp_it  /  sum_i T_i        = -0.057778802
  sequence-level normalization would instead average per-sequence means:
     pg = (1/N) sum_i A_i * (sum_t logp_it / T_i)        = -0.048160374
  lengths this batch: [128, 128, 116, 114, 128, 128, 125, 115]
  Token-level weighting means a LONG rollout contributes proportionally more gradient
  than a short one with the same advantage. If correct answers are systematically
  longer (they usually are - more reasoning steps), token-level normalization
  quietly amplifies a length preference on top of the correctness signal. This is the
  same structural bias as DPO's summed log-probs (W4/01 section 6), arriving by a
  different route, and it is why response length must be logged next to reward.

==================================================================================================
5. WHAT nanochat OMITS FROM GRPO / PPO (from the file's own docstring, verified in code)
==================================================================================================
  component                          PPO/GRPO                   nanochat                          
  ---------------------------------- -------------------------- ----------------------------------
  KL to a reference policy           trust region term          ABSENT - no reference model loaded
  importance ratio + clip            pi_new/pi_old, clipped     ABSENT - strictly on-policy       
  advantage normalization            (r-mu)/sigma  z-score      (r-mu) only, no sigma divide      
  normalization level                sequence-level             token-level (DAPO style)          
  value network / GAE                critic estimates V(s)      ABSENT - group mean is the baseline
  multiple epochs per batch          yes, hence the ratio       one pass, so no ratio is needed   

  CONSEQUENCES, not just differences:
   - No KL and no clip means NOTHING bounds how far the policy moves from the SFT model. The
     only brakes are the LR and the horizon. Capability retention (W3/02-style pretraining
     bpb) must therefore be measured explicitly; RL here can silently destroy the base model.
   - Dropping the sigma divide keeps the update magnitude proportional to how much reward
     actually varies. With binary rewards and group size k, sigma is tiny whenever the group
     is nearly unanimous, and dividing by it would explode those updates. This is a
     deliberate stability trade: less normalized, but far less spiky.
   - Strictly on-policy means every gradient step needs fresh rollouts, which is why the RL
     stage is dominated by GENERATION time, not backward time.
